Pau

The screenshots you provided are of some help.  When you invoke install.packages("Distance")​, the CRAN mirror being used (`cran.rstudio.com <http://cran.rstudio.com>`) does not find the binary version of Distance 1.0.7, but rather reverts to the source version. It is in the compilation of this source version that you run into the errors you report.

Contrast the dialog for your session:

with this dialog from my attempt to do the same thing:

> install.packages("Distance")
Installing package into ‘C:/Users/erexs/Documents/R/win-library/4.1’
(as ‘lib’ is unspecified)
trying URL 'https://cran.rstudio.com/bin/windows/contrib/4.3/Distance_1.0.7.zip'
Content type 'application/zip' length 384822 bytes (375 KB)
downloaded 375 KB

package ‘Distance’ successfully unpacked and MD5 sums checked

Downloading the binary (in zip form) avoids the compilation step, thereby resulting in successful installation.

The real question is therefore, how to convince R to install from binary rather than installing from source? I believe this is to do with the version of R you are using. According to the screenshot, you are using R 4.0.4, released in February 2021. At that time, there was no version 1.0.7 of the Distance package. Hence, there is no binary version of 1.0.7 on the CRAN mirror associated with R 4.0; this is likely why your installation request is being shunted to the source rather than binary. Similarly, when you experimented with your colleague's computer ("Users/Virginia"), her machine was using an even older version of R (R 3.6), resulting in the same installation attempt from source, rather than from binary; with the same result.

I suggest the remedy to the problem is to update the version of R on your computer. The most recent release is R 4.3.0 and the appropriate binary version of the Distance package 1.0.7 (as well as the mrds package) is located in the CRAN mirror URL associated with this R release:

https://cran.rstudio.com/bin/windows/contrib/4.3/

See how this works for you.

